  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Protecting and assisting refugees and asylum seekers, migrants and trafficking victims</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Israeli economy makes use of very large numbers of migrant workers (estimated number 160,000 at last check) who are brought into the country to work in specified sectors and for a named employer.   This leaves them vulnerable to, and often victims of, violations such as underpayment, debt bondage and subhuman living conditions, and can easily become &quot;illegal&quot; as a result of employer negligence or fraud.   Around 60,000 African asylum seekers, mostly Sudanese or Eritrean, are denied refugee status or a proper hearing, and are liable to indefinite detention.  A significant proportion of the asylum seekers, and some migrants, are also victims of trafficking, enslavement and torture.  Hotline for Refugees and Migrants (HRM) extends a comprehensive assistance and protection service for these large groups and for all other refugees and asylum seekers, migrants and trafficking victims.  Services include a telephone hotline, regular visits to detention centres, provision of information, legal advice and representation, data collection and monitoring of authorities&apos; treatment of migrant workers, High Court petitions and promoting enforcement of the rule of law, campaigning and advocacy work with decision-makers, media, non-governmental agencies and the general public.</narrative>
  </description>
